Ported from a Backspace module of the same name.   I believe this code 
to be in the public domain.

Random star colors use an illustrative display mix: red 70%, orange 15%,
yellow 8%, white 5%, blue-white 1.8%, and blue 0.2%. This reduces the red
share from the earlier population-based palette; it is not a measured sample
of stars visible from Earth. All six colors can also be selected individually.

"COSMOS mode" is optional and OFF by default. Enabling it selects slow,
depth-based travel, small muted stars, and two large diffuse red nebulae with
dark dust lanes. These foreground clouds are a cinematic addition, not a
catalog-derived population or a reproduction of footage. They appear even
when "Show moving nebulae" is off. Turning COSMOS off restores faster,
accelerating stars and the brighter palette. Mode changes apply immediately
and persist, without changing the other controls' saved choices.

"Show moving nebulae" independently controls the smaller, catalog-inspired
clouds and remains off by default. "Rotate space view" defaults off for new
settings. When enabled, COSMOS uses a slow roll (about 29 minutes per revolution
at 30 fps); ordinary mode uses the original faster roll. The speed slider
controls both modes. All options persist. There is no
background photograph or network request. Cached RGBA textures (128 pixels for small clouds, 256 for red clouds) use
warped, multiscale noise, irregular gas density, dust absorption lanes, and muted
warm emission with localized cool regions. Each cloud has its own position,
depth, radius, and orientation. Perspective uses 1/z for both position and size;
clouds share the stars' vanishing point and roll, expanding as they approach.
They fade in at the far plane, fade out near the camera, and are replaced when
they leave the view. Each slot reuses its generated texture with a new position,
size, and orientation. The bounded texture pool survives nebula toggles and resizing; changing COSMOS
style rebuilds it
and is released with the view. Animation clears only old star positions and
old/new rotated cloud bounds, then composites clouds once and redraws stars.
Cloud drawing is clipped to those bounds; full redraws remain for exposure,
resize, and enabling/disabling nebulae. Colors
represent enhanced astronomical imaging, not naked-eye surface brightness.

Prevalence model and limits:
The WISE Galactic H II catalog v2 contains approximately 8,000 H II regions and
candidates. Dividing by 41,253 square degrees gives a reference all-sky density
of 0.194 objects per square degree. The illustrative field has a 6-degree short
axis and an aspect-dependent long axis capped at 12 degrees. A Poisson draw
sets the small-cloud population (mean 12.4 at 16:9), capped at 30 to reserve
two slots for the optional cinematic red clouds. Slots are
recycled to keep that population steady during flight. Initial depths are
sampled proportional to z squared, as for a uniform volume within a cone.

This is a catalog-informed visualization, not a measured ratio to the 200
animated stars. The WISE sample is infrared-selected, includes candidates,
is concentrated toward the Galactic plane, and does not establish optical
visibility or a uniform sky distribution. The field size, flight speed, cloud
sizes, and enhanced colors are artistic choices. The all-sky mean deliberately
avoids pretending to simulate a particular Galactic sightline. All clouds are
H II-inspired; no unsupported percentages for other nebula types are assigned.
Source: https://www.ipac.caltech.edu/doi/10.26131/IRSA146
Paper: Anderson et al. (2014), The WISE Catalog of Galactic H II Regions,
https://doi.org/10.1088/0067-0049/212/1/1

GNUstep regression checks (from the project directory):

  make -C Space2View.bproj -f GNUmakefile GNUSTEP_MAKEFILES="$(gnustep-config --variable=GNUSTEP_MAKEFILES)"
  clang $(gnustep-config --objc-flags) Space2View.bproj/Tests/NebulaTests.m UtilFunctions.c -o /tmp/innerspace-nebula-tests $(gnustep-config --gui-libs)
  xvfb-run -a /tmp/innerspace-nebula-tests "$PWD/Space2View.bproj/Space2View.InnerSpace"

The test checks the default, toggle, restoration, 600 animation frames, resizing,
removal, perspective motion, catalog density, expanded palette, texture reuse,
rotation controls, and COSMOS default/persistence/red-cloud behavior. Render samples go to /tmp/innerspace-nebula*.png.
